-- title:
--   Zero ideal sheaf of a section of an invertible module is locally principal
-- statement:
--   Let $X$ be a scheme, let $M$ be an $\mathcal{O}_X$-module on $X$ satisfying `Scheme.Modules.IsInvertible`, i.e. every point of $X$ has an open neighbourhood $U$ for which the pullback of $M$ along the inclusion $U \hookrightarrow X$ is isomorphic to the unit module on $U$, and let $s \colon \mathbf{1} \to M$ be a morphism from the unit object of $X$-modules, that is, a global section of $M$. For an open $U$ and a morphism $\varphi \colon M|_U \to \mathcal{O}_U$ of modules on $U$, `coeff s U φ` denotes the element of $\Gamma(X, U)$ obtained by applying $\varphi$ over $\top$ to the restriction of $s(1)$ to $U$ and transporting along the canonical identification $\Gamma(U,\top) \cong \Gamma(X,U)$; `coeffIdeal s U` is the ideal of $\Gamma(X,U)$ spanned by all such elements, as $\varphi$ ranges over all morphisms $M|_U \to \mathcal{O}_U$; and `zeroSchemeIdeal s` is the infimum, in the lattice of ideal sheaf data on $X$, of those $J$ with `coeffIdeal s U` $\le$ $J$'s ideal at $U$ for every affine open $U$. The assertion is twofold. First, this infimum itself satisfies the defining inequality: for every affine open $V$ of $X$, `coeffIdeal s V` is contained in the ideal of `zeroSchemeIdeal s` at $V$. Second, for every affine open $U$ and every isomorphism $\tau \colon M|_U \cong \mathcal{O}_U$ of modules on $U$, the ideal of `zeroSchemeIdeal s` at $U$ equals the principal ideal generated by `coeff s U τ.hom`, and also equals `coeffIdeal s U`.
--
--   This is the basic local description of the zero scheme $Z(s)$ of a section of a line bundle: its ideal sheaf is, on any affine open over which the bundle is trivialised, the principal ideal generated by the image of the section under the trivialisation, and it agrees there with the ideal generated by all coefficients $\varphi(s|_U)$. It is the workhorse behind the further properties of $Z(s)$ used in the relative Picard functor material, such as the compatibility of `zeroSchemeIdeal` with pullback, the non-vanishing of a section at the generic point, and the existence of nonzero pulled-back sections.
